 for  black because it stops a lot of the  threats that white has you have the  knight here on c3 can't come to b5 this  b5 square is a critical square  in this defense this other knight can't  come to b5  and this bishop here on f1 can't come to  b5 so it stops a lot of the threats that  white  has and white's gonna have to come up  with a  another game plan now what's nice about  something like  the code chess is you can use this to  actually analyze this position  so if i run this position through the  code chess you can see  first it has the major threats now in a  chess opening you don't always see  immediate threats we're not too many  moves into the game  but i do still always like to look at  any position and see what the major  threats are f3 bishop to e3  not really going to be worried about  those too much but you can see it says  the best move is  a6 in this position and why is that  some of the reasons is because it  prevents the knight coming to b5 the  knight on the d  file coming to d5 and the bishop coming  to  b5 with check so as you're analyzing  your own games trying to figure out did  i miss anything  try to use this and see what moves are  preventing my opponent from doing  something and i'll start to open your  mind  into don't just come up with your own  strategy how do you stop your opponent  from doing what they're trying to do  here's another common  chess opening for whites you can see  already castled on the king side rook  here to e1 protecting the pawn  two minor pieces already involved into  the action while trying to figure out  the game  plan from here and one of those ways to  continue is  to look at prophylactic moves what are  blacks threats here  one of those threats is to get this  light square bishop involved into the  game you can see it's the only minor  piece that hasn't moved yet and where's  the best squares for that i don't  necessarily  think it's d7 or e6  it's not going to come here to f5 really  the best square for the bishop is going  to come down to  g4 pinning this knight down to the queen  and so white has the prophylactic move  of pawn to h3  that doesn't improve the central control  of  the board from white as it may seem but  really  this allows white to still hold on to  the knight which does in turn actually  hold on to the center of the board so  with these  it's these subtle moves that you need to  be thinking about we're going over chess  openings we'll go over some  some later uh examples as well  and as the previous example you can also  look at the code chess if you want  to plug in something like this example  you can see what are the major threats  in in this example  g4 bishop to g4 is something to think  about  that's what black is threatening and you  can see the best move that they  recommend  they're using the stockfish engine is  pawn to h3  you can see it prevents the bishop here  coming down to  g4 so it stops that square remember you  can analyze  entire games or if you just want to look  at positions you can do that as well  does a really good job of walking  through every single move  and what are the threats and how can you  prevent that  we can take the same ideas and apply  that to the middle game  as well it's white's move here and you  can see in this position that white has  a spatial advantage meaning  that their pieces are pushed up a little  bit higher and black's starting to run  out of room to move their pieces now  from black's standpoint the best way to  counter this  is start to exchange off some pieces and  really open  things up and that really takes away the  threats  that white has of putting too much  pressure and kind of  cramping the position for black so  that's what black's trying  to do but it's white's move and so white  has an opportunity to stop that  and if you start to look at what are  black's ideas here well really black  would like to blow up the center of the  board and maybe this pawn chain over  here  so e5 seems like a move but black's  not going to be doing that because white  has pawn takes here on e5  protected by the knight and the queen  but black also has the threat of pawn to  c5 this opens up for the light square  bishop  to attack both the knight here and just  control this long light square diagonal  and also just attacks these pawns on  the queen side of the board this pawn  here on c5 is  protected by the bishop the pawn and the  queen right here  that's a pretty good move and so white  can look at this  understand the threat that black has and  then stop that by playing the move  pawn to c5 this light square bishop now  is not really going to be going anywhere  this pawn chain is  very solid so this dark square bishop is  not going to be moving anywhere right  now  yes anytime you move you do leave some  space behind so this knight can come  to d5 but then after knight to e5 from  white i feel like white has a much  better setup here black is still cramped  maybe more cramped than before  white can easily get the light squared  bishop it's already attacking the h7  square can get the queen involved into  the game can easily maneuver this dark  square bishop over to the king side  a lot of threats that white has on this  board  while still keeping black at bay because  we recognize what that immediate threat  is  same thing as we looked at before if you  look at the code chest you can plug this  in and it shows you that exact same  thing what are the major threats from  black  well the major threat is that black  plays pawn to c5 and so what's a good  move from white  it is to play this pawn to c5 it  prevents them playing from pawn to c5  gives you some additional things as well  to  think about so this is this is how you  should be thinking about every position  whether it's in the opening whether it's  in the middle game  and then we'll now look at one in the  end game as well  the last example we're going to look at  is a prophylactic move in the end game  many times players have a dominant  position  and it all just crumbles because they're  always looking for  the best attack and they never really  stop to think  how can my opponent come back what is  the best move for them  and how should i stop them knowing that  if as long as i don't screw this up i'm  probably going to win  the game in this position it's white's  move  and white has a dominant position this  rook here on  a8 for black isn't doing anything  white has this rook here on c7 a great  square for  this rook here pretty much blocking this  king here on  the eighth rank here this queen on e3  doing a good job of not only protecting  the knight here  on f4 but also indirectly attacking this  pawn here on  h6 but why can get pretty greedy here  why could  try to gobble up more material let's say  they play rook  to b7 white was not able to  think about what is the immediate threat  that black has  and that is queen 2 a4 queen a4 attacks  both the knight here on  f4 which is being attacked by now the  queen and the rook here on f8  as well as attacks this rook here on  d1 there's also some  other ideas that if black really moves  this queen away  well now things get pretty tricky  because there's a discovered  attack here with the bishop attacking  the queen  and this is check as well so the queen  can move that would just be a huge  blunder  but thinking about where can my opponent  go if i move  is definitely important to think about  and when you factor all that in  then it comes down to okay i know my  opponent's looking to play  queen to a4 that's an immediate threat  here of attacking both the  rook here and the knight here and what  is  the best move for white to continue and  then as you analyze the position  more you realize that the move rook to  f1  is a really good defensive move  this pawn on f2 is  eventually going to be attacked by the  queen and the rook because you want to  get your knight involved maybe  knight takes here on g6 at some point  but you don't want to expose this pawn  here on f2  so this is a defensive move but it's a  prophylactic moves it  keeps you in a strong position and  doesn't really give up anything it  doesn't allow your opponent's immediate  threats  to really do anything and so last time  we'll come over to decode chess if you  look at that and you plug it in you can  see what are the immediate threats well  queen to a4  is the immediate threat that you need to  deal with and it kind of tells you  why what happens if they can play that  and it tells you what the best option is  f1 is really the preferred play in this  position  and it tells you why and if you're  thinking you know why don't i play some  other moves  it gives you some other alternatives and  explains why  f1 is better  so that is prophylactic moves hopefully  you can take some of this and apply it  to your own games always be thinking  about what your opponent is looking to  do  and how you can stop them from doing  that it's going to make you a much  better chess player  again if you haven't checked out  thecodechess.com i'll have a link in the  description below  you can sign up for free so not asking  you to go and  pay any money definitely go check it out  though see if you like it you can run  their software a couple times a day for  free you can definitely sign up for more  than that  if you want to but definitely check it  out thank you so much to the sponsors of  this video thank you to everyone for  watching the video hopefully you enjoyed  this chess strategy going over specific  tactics on how you should approach the  game  if there's other tactics or strategies  you want me to include in a future video  feel free to let me know  but thank you guys so much for watching  i'll see you in the next video

